### B.2 What Exactly Is JavaScript?
**[37:02 – 40:12]**

**Definition (from the lecture document):**
> JavaScript is a **high-level, human-readable programming language**, originally designed to run in web browsers, that controls the *behavior* of a web application.

Let's unpack each term:

- **"High-level" language:** Closer to human thinking/language than to raw machine instructions (0s and 1s). You can read JS code and roughly understand what it's doing, unlike low-level machine code.
- **"Programming language" (not markup):** It has its own syntax and rules for writing logic (loops, conditions, functions) — unlike HTML, which is a *markup* language just describing structure, with no logic.

**What JavaScript is NOT (common beginner confusions clarified):**

| Misconception | Reality |
|---|---|
| "JavaScript is related to Java" | **False.** The name was purely a **marketing decision** — Java was popular in 1995, so naming it "JavaScript" borrowed that popularity. They share almost nothing technically. |
| "JavaScript is a markup language like HTML" | **False.** HTML already handles structure well (that's why there's no "HTML6" — HTML5 is sufficient); JS is a separate, logic-capable language. |
| "JavaScript only runs in the browser" | **False** (today). It *originally* was built only for browsers, but today it also runs outside the browser (via Node.js — covered in B.4). |

> **Analogy for "high-level":** Reading assembly/machine code is like reading raw electrical wiring diagrams. Reading JavaScript is like reading a recipe written in plain English — you (a human) can follow it without being an electrical engineer.

---

### B.3 A Brief History of JavaScript
**[40:31 – 43:38]**

| Fact | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Created by** | Brendan Eich |
| **Year** | 1995 |
| **Time taken to build it** | Just **10 days** |
| **For which browser?** | Netscape (a dominant browser at the time) |
| **Original goal** | Make static web pages *interactive* |
| **Why the name "JavaScript"?** | Marketing — to piggyback on Java's popularity at the time. Not because of any technical relationship to Java. |

**Why mention "created in 10 days"?**
The instructor flags this deliberately: a language built in 10 days will naturally have **inconsistent rules, quirks, and bugs** baked into its design. This becomes important later when understanding *why* JavaScript needs a strict standard (see B.5) — different browsers implementing a hastily-designed language independently would cause chaos without one.

Despite the rushed origin, JavaScript succeeded because it was **supported by every browser** (Netscape, later Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge, etc.) and executed consistently enough across them to become the universal choice for web interactivity.

