### B.4 Where Does JavaScript Run?
**[43:38 – 50:08]**

This is one of the most important conceptual sections — it directly answers a student's confusion (Ganesh's question about "do we need a browser to run JS?").

#### B.4.1 Running JavaScript INSIDE the Browser (the "Frontend"/"Client-side")

**How does a browser "understand" JavaScript code?**
Every browser has a built-in **JavaScript Engine** — a program that reads JS code and executes it. Different browsers use different engines:

| Browser | JavaScript Engine |
|---|---|
| Google Chrome | **V8** |
| Mozilla Firefox | **SpiderMonkey** |
| Safari | (Apple's own JS engine) |

**Why does this matter?**
Since each browser company built its *own* engine independently, there's a risk that the **same JS code could behave differently** on different browsers if there's no common set of rules. (This directly sets up the need for ECMAScript standards, B.5.)

**What else does the browser provide besides the engine?**
- **The DOM (Document Object Model)** — explained in detail in B.4.3 below.
- **Timers, Events, Storage** — mentioned as topics to be covered in future sessions; not explained in depth here.

> ⚠️ **Term check — "Client-side":** When JS runs *inside* the browser, this is called **frontend** or **client-side**, because the browser represents the *client* (the user's machine/software) in a client-server relationship.

#### B.4.2 Running JavaScript OUTSIDE the Browser — Node.js

**ELI5:** Just like you need to *install Python* on your computer before you can run `.py` files outside of any special tool, you need to install something called **Node.js** before you can run JavaScript code outside a browser.

**Technical definition:**
> **Node.js is NOT a framework or a library of JavaScript.** It is a **runtime environment** — software installed on your machine that allows JavaScript to execute outside the browser.

| Term | What it means | Analogy given in lecture |
|---|---|---|
| **Runtime (Node.js)** | An environment/"vessel" that allows a language to execute on a machine | Like the Python interpreter — you must install it before running Python code locally |
| **Framework (e.g., Vue.js, React, Angular)** | A tool/library *built using* JavaScript, that itself needs to run *on top of* something (a browser, or Node.js) | These are like apps you install on top of an operating system |
| **NPM (Node Package Manager)** | A tool bundled with Node.js to install/manage JavaScript packages | Directly compared to **pip** in Python — pip manages Python packages, NPM manages Node.js/JavaScript packages |

> **Naming convention clue:** Any tool/framework name ending in `.js` (e.g., Vue.js, Express.js, React — sort of) usually signals "this is a JavaScript-based framework/library." **Node.js itself is the exception** — despite the `.js` suffix, it is *not* a framework; it's the runtime.

**Why does Node.js matter? (Significance)**
Once JS can run outside the browser (via Node.js):
- It can be used to **build servers** — just like Python (via Flask) can.
- It gains **file system access** — like Python can read/write local files, JS running via Node.js can too (this is *not* possible for JS running inside a browser — see B.4.4 below on sandboxing).
- JavaScript effectively becomes a **general-purpose language** usable for *both* frontend (in-browser) and backend (via Node.js) — one language, two use-cases. This is a major reason for its popularity.

**Student clarifications captured in this segment:**
- *[Ganesh, 1:03:56]:* "Is JS also line-by-line executed/interpreted like Python?" → **Yes**, both are **dynamically typed** interpreted-style languages (executed, not compiled ahead-of-time the way C/C++ is).
- *[Aakarsh, 1:04:36]:* "Is NPM like a terminal, similar to pip in Python?" → NPM is Node's **package manager**, directly analogous to pip.
- *[Krithik, 1:05:09]:* "Is Node.js's runtime concept similar to a Colab notebook connecting to a runtime?" → Partially — a **Colab notebook is a larger tool** (an IDE-like environment) that happens to *also* connect to an underlying runtime for the language you're using. Node.js itself is just the runtime piece, not the whole IDE.

#### B.4.3 What Is the DOM? (Document Object Model)
**[44:53 – 45:39]**

**ELI5:** Imagine your HTML page as a family tree. There's one "root" ancestor (usually the `<html>` tag), and everyone else is a descendant — parents, children, grandchildren. The DOM is just that family tree, but for HTML elements.

**Technical definition:**
> The DOM is a **tree-like structure of nodes** representing every element in an HTML document — showing which element is the root, which are its children, grandchildren, and so on.

**Why does the DOM matter for JavaScript?**
Because **JavaScript can directly access and modify DOM elements** — this is *how* JS achieves the "behavior" layer described in B.1. If JS couldn't "see" the DOM's structure, it couldn't change what's on the page.

#### B.4.4 The Browser as a Sandbox (Security Boundary)
**[36:52 – 44:20, revisited 1:36:19 – 1:38:01]**

**Key restriction:** JavaScript running **inside a browser CANNOT access your local files** (e.g., it can't just open and read a file on your hard drive).

**Why?**
> The browser acts as a **sandbox** for JavaScript. Even though the browser itself runs on your machine (and *could* theoretically access your files, camera, etc.), the browser deliberately restricts what the JavaScript running inside it is allowed to do.

**Student question [1:37:27]:** *"Sometimes Chrome asks permission to access my camera or file system — doesn't that mean JS can access my local system?"*
→ **Answer:** Even then, it's the **browser** requesting and holding that access, not JavaScript directly. For JS/a web app's code to use such access, **specific, explicit permissions** must be granted through the browser's own permission system — JS never gets unrestricted local access on its own.

> ⚠️ **Remember This (exam-relevant distinction):**
> - JS **inside a browser** → sandboxed, no direct file system access.
> - JS **outside a browser via Node.js** → full file system access, just like Python.

