### C.2 Method 1 — Embedding JavaScript Directly Inside HTML (Internal Script)
**[1:09:50 – 1:12:40]**

**The boilerplate HTML used:**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<body>
  <h1>Hello</h1>

  <script>
    console.log("Hello, welcome to the world");
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Line-by-line breakdown:**

| Code piece | What it does |
|---|---|
| `<!DOCTYPE html>` | Tells the browser "this document is HTML5" — standard boilerplate, not JavaScript-specific. |
| `<h1>Hello</h1>` | A regular HTML heading element — just structure, no behavior yet. |
| `<script> ... </script>` | An HTML tag that tells the browser "everything inside here is **JavaScript code**, not HTML." This is the **internal/embedded** way of adding JS. |
| `console.log("...")` | A JavaScript **function call**. It sends ("logs") the text inside the parentheses to the browser's **console** (a special developer-tools output panel — not the same as printing on the actual webpage). |

**Breaking down `console.log(...)` itself:**
- `console` → a **built-in object** that every browser provides automatically (you never have to define or import it — it's just always available in the browser environment, similar to how `print` is always available in Python without importing anything).
- `.log(...)` → a **method** (a function that belongs to the `console` object) that writes/outputs whatever is inside the parentheses.
- `"Hello, welcome to the world"` → a **string** (text data) — the actual message being logged.
- `;` (semicolon) → marks the **end of a statement** in JavaScript. (Convention used throughout the lecture, though JS can sometimes work without it — the instructor didn't go into "automatic semicolon insertion" nuances at this stage.)

**Analogy used in lecture:** `console.log()` is JavaScript's equivalent of Python's `print()` — except instead of printing to a **terminal** (a system-level tool), it prints to the browser's **console** (a browser-level tool, only visible via Developer Tools).

**How to actually see the output:**
1. Save the file.
2. Open it in the browser (instructor used the **Live Server** VS Code extension to auto-launch/refresh it).
3. Right-click the page → **Inspect** → open the **Console** tab in Developer Tools.
4. You'll see: `Hello, welcome to the world` — along with a file/line reference like `index.html:6`, showing you exactly *which line* produced that log — very useful for debugging.

> **ELI5 recap:** Think of the browser console as a hidden "backstage" screen only developers look at — regular users browsing the site never see it unless they open Developer Tools.

