### C.3 Method 2 — External Script File
**[1:16:41 – 1:20:00]**

**Why this method exists (the problem with Method 1):**
Writing lots of JavaScript directly inside HTML `<script>` tags becomes messy as your code grows — HTML is meant to describe *structure*, not host large amounts of logic. This is the same reasoning as **why CSS is usually kept in a separate `.css` file** — a principle called **separation of concerns**.

**Step-by-step: creating and linking an external script**

**1. Create a new file: `script.js`**
```javascript
console.log("From external script");
```
- File extension **`.js`** signals to tools/editors that this is a JavaScript file.
- Same `console.log()` function as before — this line, when loaded, will print its message to the console too.

**2. Link it inside `index.html`:**
```html
<script src="./script.js"></script>
```

**Breaking down this line:**
| Part | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `<script ...></script>` | Same script tag as before — but this time it has **no code written directly inside it**. |
| `src="..."` | An **attribute** (short for "source") telling the browser: "Don't look for code between these tags — instead, go fetch the actual JavaScript code from this file path." |
| `./script.js` | A **relative file path**. `./` means "look in the current folder" (the same folder the `index.html` file is sitting in). `script.js` is the filename to fetch. |

**Result after reload:** Both console messages appear —
```
Hello, welcome to the world     (from index.html:6, the internal script)
From external script            (from script.js:1, the external file)
```

> **Three ways to add JS to HTML, as shown in the instructor's editor autocomplete [1:17:51]:**
> 1. `<script>` — for internal/embedded code (no attributes).
> 2. `<script type="module">` — deferred to a future session, not explained here.
> 3. `<script src="...">` — for loading external files.

> ⚠️ **Remember This:** In real projects (including your Vue.js work from Week 3 onward), you will **always** use external `.js` files, not embedded scripts — this demo's Method 1 is purely for teaching purposes.

