### C.4 Where to Place the `<script>` Tag: Head vs. Just Before `</body>`
**[1:20:34 – 1:23:39]**

**Student question [Dheeraj, 1:21:07]:** *"Can we put the script in the `<head>` instead?"*

**Answer — yes, technically both work, but the *order of execution* changes:**

- If `<script>` is placed in `<head>` → **it runs before the rest of the HTML body has even loaded/rendered.**
- If `<script>` is placed **just before `</body>`** (i.e., at the very end of the HTML) → **the HTML content renders first, and the script runs last.**

**Why does this matter? (The real-world reasoning)**
- With a *tiny* one-line script (like this demo), the difference is invisible.
- But in a real app, your `<script>` might contain **a lot of logic** that takes time to process (adding click behavior to 10–15 elements, etc.).
- As a *user*, you'd rather see the page's content (text, images, buttons) appear immediately — even if the buttons aren't "clickable" yet for a split second — than stare at a **blank white screen** while a large script finishes loading.
- **Best practice, therefore:** Place custom `<script>` tags **at the bottom of `<body>`**, right before `</body>`, so that:
  1. All HTML elements mount/render first (structure + look appear instantly).
  2. Then JavaScript attaches behavior on top of the already-visible page.

> **Analogy:** Imagine unpacking furniture into a new house. You'd rather have all the furniture placed in the room first (HTML/CSS), even if you haven't plugged in the smart-features (JS) yet, rather than making guests wait outside until every smart plug is configured.

> ⚠️ **Remember This (exam-relevant):** This is about **order of execution**, not "precedence" or importance — the instructor explicitly corrected this terminology when a student used the word "precedence."

