### C.7 DOM Manipulation — Changing the Page with JavaScript
**[1:31:15 – 1:36:33]**

This is the payoff section — showing JavaScript actually *doing* something to the page, tying back to the DOM concept from Part B.4.3.

**Code used:**
```javascript
document.body.style.backgroundColor = "lightblue";
```

**Breaking this down piece by piece (exactly as taught):**

| Piece | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `document` | A **built-in object** (like `console`) representing the *entire loaded HTML file*. The moment you write `document`, JavaScript has access to everything in that HTML — `body`, `head`, every element, including your `<script>` tag itself. |
| `.body` | Accesses the **`<body>` element** specifically, out of everything `document` contains. |
| `.style` | Accesses the **style properties** of that body element (equivalent to what you'd control via CSS). |
| `.backgroundColor` | The specific CSS property being changed — background color. |
| `= "lightblue"` | **Assignment** — sets that property's value to the string `"lightblue"`. |

**Putting it together (plain English):**
> "Go to the whole document → find the body element → access its style object → set its backgroundColor property to lightblue."

**Result:** The entire page background turns light blue — achieved **without writing any CSS at all**. This demonstrates that JavaScript can reach into the DOM and change visual/structural properties directly, live, based on logic — something plain HTML/CSS alone cannot do (they're static once written).

#### C.7.1 Camel Case vs. Kebab Case — An Important Naming Convention
**[1:33:11 – 1:34:33]**

This is a subtle but **very commonly tested/confused** point:

| Context | Naming style | Example |
|---|---|---|
| **CSS** (writing `background-color` in a stylesheet or inline `style="..."`) | **Kebab-case** — words separated by hyphens | `background-color` |
| **JavaScript** (accessing the same property via the DOM, e.g. `.style.___`) | **Camel-case** — no hyphens, each new word capitalized (except the first) | `backgroundColor` |

**Why does this conversion exist?**
- Hyphens (`-`) have a **special meaning in JavaScript** — they represent the **subtraction operator**. If JavaScript allowed `background-color` as a property name, the language couldn't tell whether you meant "a property called background-color" or "background minus color" (an arithmetic operation). To avoid this ambiguity, JavaScript's convention for accessing CSS-style properties **always converts hyphenated CSS names into camelCase.**

> ⚠️ **Remember This (very testable):** *Any* CSS property with a hyphen (e.g., `font-size`, `border-radius`, `background-color`) must be written in **camelCase** when accessed/set via JavaScript (`fontSize`, `borderRadius`, `backgroundColor`). This rule applies consistently across JavaScript — including later in Vue.js, per the instructor's note.

#### C.7.2 A Live Experiment: CSS vs. JS — Who Wins?
**[1:34:39 – 1:35:23]**

**Setup:** The instructor set the `<body>` element's background color via inline HTML/CSS (e.g., `style="background-color: yellow"`) **and** via the JavaScript line above (`lightblue`) — at the same time, on the same page.

**Student question [Dheeraj]:** *"If they're different, which one takes effect?"*

**Result: JavaScript's value (`lightblue`) wins — it overrides the original CSS value (`yellow`).**

**Why?**
> Because of **execution order** (tying back to Section C.4): the CSS/HTML is parsed and applied first as the page loads, but the `<script>` (placed at the bottom of `<body>`, per best practice) runs **afterward** — and since it runs later, its changes to `document.body.style.backgroundColor` **overwrite** whatever value was there before.

> **Key insight:** This demonstrates a general truth in web development — **whichever code runs *last* wins**, when two different sources try to set the same property on the same element.

