### 🔑 Part C Summary & Key Takeaways

- JS must be loaded via HTML into the browser — either **embedded** (`<script>...code...</script>`) or **external** (`<script src="file.js"></script>`).
- **External files** are the professional/scalable choice — this is what you'll use from Vue.js onward.
- **`console.log()`** → logs to the hidden browser console (dev tools only), like Python's `print()` but to a different destination.
- **`alert()`** → shows a visible, **blocking** popup — pauses the rest of the page's execution/loading until dismissed. This is a first hint of **sync vs. async** behavior (deferred to future sessions).
- **Script placement matters**: put custom scripts **just before `</body>`** so HTML/CSS render first, and JS attaches behavior afterward — better perceived performance.
- **DOM manipulation** (`document.body.style.backgroundColor = "..."`) lets JS change the page live, without touching CSS files.
- **CSS uses kebab-case** (`background-color`); **JavaScript uses camelCase** (`backgroundColor`) for the exact same property, because hyphens are reserved as JS's subtraction operator.
- **Whichever code runs last wins** when multiple sources try to set the same style property.
- JavaScript **cannot** access local files when running inside a browser — the browser sandboxes it; only Node.js (outside the browser) grants file system access.

### ⚠️ Common Mistakes (Part C)
- Forgetting that `console.log()` output is **only visible in Developer Tools**, not on the actual visible webpage — beginners often think nothing happened.
- Writing CSS-style hyphenated property names directly in JavaScript (`document.body.style.background-color`) — this is a **syntax error**; must be `backgroundColor`.
- Placing large/behavior-heavy scripts in `<head>` without realizing it can delay the visible rendering of the page.
- Assuming `alert()` behaves exactly like `console.log()` (non-blocking) — it does not; it **pauses** everything until dismissed.
- Confusing execution **order** with **precedence/importance** — the instructor explicitly corrected this distinction (Section C.4).

